    Cleanup Logs - Installation and Usage
    
    Files created:
    - /workspace/cleanup-logs.sh  (script created by the agent)
    
    Summary:
    This script removes files under a log directory older than the configured retention period.
    Defaults: logdir=/var/logs, retention=7 days.
    The script includes safety checks:
    - --dry-run to show what would be removed
    - Skips files currently in use (uses lsof or fuser if available)
    - Supports --exclude PATTERN to skip specific files
    
    Quick test (locally):
    1. Prepare a test directory and files:
       mkdir -p /tmp/testlogs && touch /tmp/testlogs/old.log && touch /tmp/testlogs/new.log && \
       # make 'old' look old
       touch -d "10 days ago" /tmp/testlogs/old.log
    
    2. Dry-run test:
       /workspace/cleanup-logs.sh --logdir /tmp/testlogs --retention 7 --dry-run
    
    3. Real run (be careful):
       sudo /workspace/cleanup-logs.sh --logdir /tmp/testlogs --retention 7
    
    Installation options (choose one):
    
    Option A - Cron entry (recommended when systemd timers aren't used)
    
    1) Copy to a system PATH and make executable:
       sudo cp /workspace/cleanup-logs.sh /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh
       sudo chmod 755 /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh
    
    2) Create a cron.d entry to run hourly (runs at minute 0 each hour):
       sudo bash -c 'cat > /etc/cron.d/cleanup-logs <<EOF
    0 * * * * root /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh --logdir /var/logs --retention 7 >> /var/log/cleanup-logs-cron.log 2>&1
    EOF'
    
    Notes: Files under /etc/cron.d require a user field (root above). Logs are appended to /var/log/cleanup-logs-cron.log (create or rotate as needed).
    
    Option B - Put script in /etc/cron.hourly (less control over exact minute):
       sudo cp /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh /etc/cron.hourly/cleanup-logs
       sudo chmod 755 /etc/cron.hourly/cleanup-logs
    
    Option C - systemd timer (recommended on systemd systems for precise control)
    
    Create /etc/systemd/system/cleanup-logs.service with:
    
    [Unit]
    Description=One-shot cleanup of old log files
    
    [Service]
    Type=oneshot
    ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh --logdir /var/logs --retention 7
    
    And /etc/systemd/system/cleanup-logs.timer:
    
    [Unit]
