     1  Cleanup Logs - Installation and Usage
     2  
     3  Files created:
     4  - /workspace/cleanup-logs.sh  (script created by the agent)
     5  
     6  Summary:
     7  This script removes files under a log directory older than the configured retention period.
     8  Defaults: logdir=/var/logs, retention=7 days.
     9  The script includes safety checks:
    10  - --dry-run to show what would be removed
    11  - Skips files currently in use (uses lsof or fuser if available)
    12  - Supports --exclude PATTERN to skip specific files
    13  
    14  Quick test (locally):
    15  1. Prepare a test directory and files:
    16     mkdir -p /tmp/testlogs && touch /tmp/testlogs/old.log && touch /tmp/testlogs/new.log && \
    17     # make 'old' look old
    18     touch -d "10 days ago" /tmp/testlogs/old.log
    19  
    20  2. Dry-run test:
    21     /workspace/cleanup-logs.sh --logdir /tmp/testlogs --retention 7 --dry-run
    22  
    23  3. Real run (be careful):
    24     sudo /workspace/cleanup-logs.sh --logdir /tmp/testlogs --retention 7
    25  
    26  Installation options (choose one):
    27  
    28  Option A - Cron entry (recommended when systemd timers aren't used)
    29  
    30  1) Copy to a system PATH and make executable:
    31     sudo cp /workspace/cleanup-logs.sh /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh
    32     sudo chmod 755 /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh
    33  
    34  2) Create a cron.d entry to run hourly (runs at minute 0 each hour):
    35     sudo bash -c 'cat > /etc/cron.d/cleanup-logs <<EOF
    36  0 * * * * root /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh --logdir /var/logs --retention 7 >> /var/log/cleanup-logs-cron.log 2>&1
    37  EOF'
    38  
    39  Notes: Files under /etc/cron.d require a user field (root above). Logs are appended to /var/log/cleanup-logs-cron.log (create or rotate as needed).
    40  
    41  Option B - Put script in /etc/cron.hourly (less control over exact minute):
    42     sudo cp /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh /etc/cron.hourly/cleanup-logs
    43     sudo chmod 755 /etc/cron.hourly/cleanup-logs
    44  
    45  Option C - systemd timer (recommended on systemd systems for precise control)
    46  
    47  Create /etc/systemd/system/cleanup-logs.service with:
    48  
    49  [Unit]
    50  Description=One-shot cleanup of old log files
    51  
    52  [Service]
    53  Type=oneshot
    54  ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh --logdir /var/logs --retention 7
    55  
    56  And /etc/systemd/system/cleanup-logs.timer:
    57  
    58  [Unit]
    59  Description=Run cleanup-logs hourly
    60  
    61  [Timer]
    62  OnCalendar=hourly
    63  Persistent=true
    64  
    65  [Install]
    66  WantedBy=timers.target
    67  
    68  Enable and start the timer:
    69     sudo systemctl daemon-reload
    70     sudo systemctl enable --now cleanup-logs.timer
    71  
    72  Safety notes & suggestions:
    73  - Start with --dry-run and small test directories before running on production.
    74  - Consider retaining compressed rotated logs instead of immediate deletion if audits/history are required.
    75  - If some logs must never be deleted, add them via --exclude patterns or update the script to use a whitelist.
    76  
    77  Support: The script is placed in /workspace. Make it available on the target server (copy to /usr/local/bin or desired location) and follow one of 
the installation options above.


