     5  
     6  Summary:
     7  This script removes files under a log directory older than the configured retention period.
     8  Defaults: logdir=/var/logs, retention=7 days.
     9  The script includes safety checks:
    10  - --dry-run to show what would be removed
    11  - Skips files currently in use (uses lsof or fuser if available)
    12  - Supports --exclude PATTERN to skip specific files
    13  
    14  Quick test (locally):
    15  1. Prepare a test directory and files:
    16     mkdir -p /tmp/testlogs && touch /tmp/testlogs/old.log && touch /tmp/testlogs/new.log && \
    17     # make 'old' look old
    18     touch -d "10 days ago" /tmp/testlogs/old.log
    19  
    20  2. Dry-run test:
    21     /workspace/cleanup-logs.sh --logdir /tmp/testlogs --retention 7 --dry-run
    22  
    23  3. Real run (be careful):
    24     sudo /workspace/cleanup-logs.sh --logdir /tmp/testlogs --retention 7
    25  
    26  Installation options (choose one):
    27  
    28  Option A - Cron entry (recommended when systemd timers aren't used)
    29  
    30  1) Copy to a system PATH and make executable:
    31     sudo cp /workspace/cleanup-logs.sh /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh
    32     sudo chmod 755 /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh
    33  
    34  2) Create a cron.d entry to run hourly (runs at minute 0 each hour). Two common variants are shown below:
    35  
    36     a) Retention-based (default behavior - safe):
    37     sudo bash -c 'cat > /etc/cron.d/cleanup-logs <<EOF
    38  0 * * * * root /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh --logdir /var/logs --retention 7 >> /var/log/cleanup-logs-cron.log 2>&1
    39  EOF'
    40  
    41     b) Force-all (DANGEROUS - removes all regular files under /var/logs every hour):
    42     Only use this if you have explicit approval. Requires double-confirm flags to run.
    43     sudo bash -c 'cat > /etc/cron.d/cleanup-logs-forceall <<EOF
    44  0 * * * * root /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh --logdir /var/logs --force-all --confirm-delete-all >> /var/log/cleanup-logs-cron.log 2>&1
    45  EOF'
    46  
    47  
    48  Notes: Files under /etc/cron.d require a user field (root above). Logs are appended to /var/log/cleanup-logs-cron.log (create or rotate as needed).
    49  
    50  Option B - Put script in /etc/cron.hourly (less control over exact minute):
    51     sudo cp /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh /etc/cron.hourly/cleanup-logs
    52     sudo chmod 755 /etc/cron.hourly/cleanup-logs
    53  
    54  Option C - systemd timer (recommended on systemd systems for precise control)
    55  
    56  Create /etc/systemd/system/cleanup-logs.service with:
    57  
    58  [Unit]
    59  Description=One-shot cleanup of old log files
    60  
    61  [Service]
