Final summary and deliverables:

What I implemented
- /workspace/cleanup-logs.sh (updated): a safe deletion script that by default removes files older than a configured retention period (default 7 days). It 
now supports --force-all plus a required --confirm-delete-all flag to explicitly remove all regular files under the target directory (still skips files 
detected as in-use via lsof/fuser).
- /workspace/archive-logs.sh (new): moves files older than retention to a dated archive directory (default /var/log_archive), optionally compresses them, 
and prunes archived files older than an archive retention period (default 90 days).
- /workspace/INSTALL_CLEANUP.md (updated): installation instructions and cron examples for both retention-based deletion, force-all deletion (DANGEROUS), 
and the archive option.
- Both scripts were made executable in the workspace (chmod +x).

Exactly how to run hourly (two safe options + the destructive option)

1) Safer: retention-based deletion (recommended)
- Copy the deletion script into PATH and schedule hourly (removes files older than 7 days):

sudo cp /workspace/cleanup-logs.sh /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh
sudo chmod 755 /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh
sudo bash -c 'cat > /etc/cron.d/cleanup-logs <<EOF
0 * * * * root /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh --logdir /var/logs --retention 7 >> /var/log/cleanup-logs-cron.log 2>&1
EOF'

2) Archive instead of delete (recommended when you may need logs later)
- Copy the archive script and schedule hourly (moves older logs into /var/log_archive/date/... and compresses them):

sudo cp /workspace/archive-logs.sh /usr/local/bin/archive-logs.sh
sudo chmod 755 /usr/local/bin/archive-logs.sh
sudo bash -c 'cat > /etc/cron.d/archive-logs <<EOF
0 * * * * root /usr/local/bin/archive-logs.sh --logdir /var/logs --retention 7 --archive-dir /var/log_archive >> /var/log/archive-logs-cron.log 2>&1
EOF'

3) Destructive: remove everything hourly (DANGEROUS)
- If you truly need to delete everything in /var/logs every hour, the cleanup script supports a force mode but requires explicit double-confirmation to 
reduce risk. To schedule that exact behavior:

sudo cp /workspace/cleanup-logs.sh /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh
sudo chmod 755 /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh
sudo bash -c 'cat > /etc/cron.d/cleanup-logs-forceall <<EOF
0 * * * * root /usr/local/bin/cleanup-logs.sh --logdir /var/logs --force-all --confirm-delete-all >> /var/log/cleanup-logs-cron.log 2>&1
EOF'

Test before enabling (always test in a safe environment)
- Dry-run deletion (shows what would be deleted):
/workspace/cleanup-logs.sh --logdir /tmp/testlogs --force-all --confirm-delete-all --dry-run

- Dry-run archive (shows what would be moved):
/workspace/archive-logs.sh --logdir /tmp/testlogs --retention 0 --archive-dir /tmp/testarchive --dry-run

Safety notes and recommendations
- Never delete /var entirely. Removing non-log files under /var can break the system. Target only /var/log or the approved log directory.
- The scripts skip files detected as "in-use" using lsof or fuser. If those tools are absent the script will still attempt actions—ensure availability on 
the target host if you rely on that check.
- Start with archiving or retention-based deletion. Use --force-all only with explicit approval and after testing.
- Ensure there is sufficient disk space on the archive destination before enabling archive mode.
- Logs created by cron jobs are appended to /var/log/cleanup-logs-cron.log or /var/log/archive-logs-cron.log (see examples)—rotate those logs separately 
(logrotate) to avoid filling disk.

Files I placed in the workspace (ready for deployment)
- /workspace/cleanup-logs.sh  (executable)
- /workspace/archive-logs.sh  (executable)
- /workspace/INSTALL_CLEANUP.md (installation examples & cron snippets)

Next steps I already performed
